$ ==========================================================================
$ CaSrO.POP  -  experimental data for PARROT, Ca-Sr-O system
$ Rewritten 2026-08-04.
$ ==========================================================================
$
$ CONSTRUCTION RULES THAT MUST BE RESPECTED (learned the hard way)
$
$ R1. CREATE_NEW_EQUILIBRIUM n,1 starts with ALL PHASES SUSPENDED.
$     Nothing needs to be suspended: the phases that must take part have
$     to be ENTERED explicitly. If none is entered, or if every one that
$     is entered goes with FIX, no phase is left with a variable amount
$     and Thermo-Calc aborts with
$        *** ERROR 1609 IN QEQUIL: NO PHASE WITH VARIABLE AMOUNT
$     Moreover, with no entered phases there are no defined components
$     either, and SET_REFERENCE_STATE fails with ERROR 1278 GZFREF: NO
$     SUCH COMPONENT. That is why every equilibrium starts with a
$     CHANGE_STATUS ... =ENTERED.
$
$ R2. conditions + fixed phases = C + 2 = 5, because the system has three
$     components (Ca, Sr, O). Fixing a phase counts as a condition.
$
$ R3. HALITE#2 is NOT entered in the equilibria where a HOMOGENEOUS
$     solution is wanted. At 298 K the miscibility gap is extremely wide,
$     so if it were entered, the enthalpy of mixing points would be
$     computed on a two-phase mixture and not on the solution.
$
$ R5. SET_ALL_START is NOT used. It asks interactively for the site
$     fractions of each sublattice and, in macro mode, swallows the
$     comment lines of the POP as if they were numbers:
$        *** ERROR 1034 IN GETREL: NO DIGIT
$     Ending it with N does not avoid this: the prompt appears anyway,
$     offering the SET_START_VALUE as the default. Instead, each
$     equilibrium carries its own explicit SET_START_VALUE, which ask
$     nothing.
$
$ R6. SET_START_VALUE is given as SITE FRACTION Y(phase,constituent),
$     not as mole fraction X(phase,component).
$
$ R7. A multi-sublattice phase CANNOT be the reference state of its own
$     components. SET_REFERENCE_STATE CA HALITE fails with ERROR 1278
$     GZFREF: NO SUCH COMPONENT because no composition of (Ca,Sr)1(O)1 is
$     "pure Ca". It only works for single-sublattice phases, such as the
$     LIQ of the tcex36 example.
$
$ R8. In an equilibrium with a miscibility gap the SET_START_VALUE are
$     NOT cosmetic: they decide whether the calculation converges. The
$     two composition sets start from the same point unless told
$     otherwise, and then they either collapse into a single phase (huge
$     residual, sum of squares in the tens of thousands) or the solver
$     just spins:
$        *** ERROR 1611 IN QTHISS: TOO MANY ITERATIONS
$     That is why TABLE_HEAD is NOT used for the gap: in a table the
$     start values are the same for every row, and here they have to
$     change with temperature. Each equilibrium is written out in full,
$     with the start values of ITS OWN temperature, taken from the
$     binodal computed with the starting parameters and displaced 0.03
$     outwards to leave room for the optimization to move the parameters
$     and with them the binodal.
$
$ R4. HALITE#2 does not exist until it is created in
$     GIBBS_ENERGY_SYSTEM before compiling. Without that, ERROR 1110
$     ILLEGAL COMPOSITION SET. The major constituents of set 1 must be
$     declared as well. Both commands open one prompt per sublattice, and
$     putting the arguments on the same line is NOT enough:
$     "composition_sets 2 SR O" leaves the sublattice 2 prompt open and
$     eats the next line. One answer per line:
$        amend_phase HALITE composition_sets 2
$        SR
$        O
$        amend_phase HALITE major 1
$        CA
$        O
$
$ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ WHAT CAN ACTUALLY BE OPTIMIZED WITH THIS FILE
$
$   L(HALITE,CA,SR:O;0) and ;1 <- yes, with AGAP and AGAPT (15 points)
$   L(LIQUID,CAO,SRO;0)        <- NO. No experimental datum on the
$                                 CaO-SrO liquid exists. Risold et al.
$                                 (1997) declare it unknown and their
$                                 value of 50000 J is a fitting parameter
$                                 of a quaternary, not a measurement.
$   L(LIQUID,SRO,O;0)          <- NO. There are no data either.
$
$ The ALIQ and AMLT blocks are VERIFICATION, not target. Before
$ optimizing, inside EDIT_EXPERIMENTS:
$     s-we 0 ALIQ
$     s-we 0 AMLT
$ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ PROVENANCE OF THE DATA
$
$ AGAP  Jacob and Jayadevan, J. Phase Equilibria 21(4) (2000) 350-356,
$       Table 1 and text. Originals from Jacob and Waseda, J. Am. Ceram.
$       Soc. 81(4) (1998) 1065-1068. x(SrO) on a cation basis 0.240 and
$       0.712; since X(HALITE,SR) counts ALL atoms and half of them are
$       oxygen, in Thermo-Calc variables they are 0.120 and 0.356.
$ AHMX  Deactivated, see the corresponding block further below.
$ AGAPT Miscibility gap between 880 and 1128 K, digitized by the author
$       from Figure 1 of Risold et al. (1997): (squares) Jacob et al. and
$       (circles) Roth. These are the data Risold fitted.
$ ALIQ  Fischbach (1985), DTA and chemical analysis, digitized from
$       Figure 3 of Deffrennes et al. (2020). These are the data that
$       work ACCEPTS; those of Zaitsev and of Bevan are discarded there.
$ AMLT  CaO 3222+-25 K (Manara et al.). SrO 2870 K, the value adopted by
$       Risold et al. (1996) among 2703, 2805, 2872 and 2938 K.
$ ==========================================================================

ENTER_SYMBOL CONSTANT P0=101325,DXG=0.0025,DXT=0.008,DXL=0.003
ENTER_SYMBOL CONSTANT DT=10

$ ==========================================================================
$ AGAP - halite miscibility gap at 1100 K
$
$ Both composition sets are entered, neither of them fixed: the overall
$ composition is placed inside the gap (X(SR) = 0.238, the midpoint of
$ 0.120 and 0.356) and the equilibrium is left to find the two branches
$ on its own. This way every phase has a variable amount and R1 holds.
$ Conditions: P, T, N, X(SR), X(O) = 5 = C+2, with no fixed phases.
$ ==========================================================================

CREATE_NEW_EQUILIBRIUM 1,1
CHANGE_STATUS PHASE HALITE#1,HALITE#2=ENTERED 1
SET-CONDITION P=P0,T=1100,N=1,X(SR)=0.238,X(O)=0.5
EXPERIMENT X(HALITE#1,SR)=0.120:DXG
EXPERIMENT X(HALITE#2,SR)=0.356:DXG
LABEL AGAP
$ Binodal computed at 1100 K: y_Sr = 0.2325 and 0.6958. See R8.
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#1,CA)=0.7975
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#1,SR)=0.2025
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#2,SR)=0.7258
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#2,CA)=0.2742

$ ==========================================================================
$ AHMX - enthalpy of mixing of the halite at 298.15 K
$
$ BLOCK DEACTIVATED. The data are documented here but are NOT compiled
$ as experiments. Reason:
$
$ For HMR to be an enthalpy of MIXING, the three components must be
$ referred to the halite itself:
$      SET_REFERENCE_STATE CA HALITE * 1E5
$ and Thermo-Calc rejects that with
$      *** ERROR 1278 IN GZFREF: NO SUCH COMPONENT
$
$ It is not a syntax error: that form is the one in the official example
$ tcex36.POP (SET_REFERENCE_STATE A LIQ * 1E5), where it works. What
$ fails is the formulation. In tcex36 the LIQ phase has ONE sublattice
$ with A and B, so "pure A in LIQ" exists. HALITE is (Ca,Sr)1(O)1: no
$ composition of the phase is "pure Ca", because it always drags the
$ oxygen along. And for component O it is worse still, because
$ sublattice 1 would have to be Ca or Sr and there is no unique choice.
$ In short, the halite cannot serve as reference state for its own
$ components.
$
$ Not much is lost: the miscibility gap between 880 and 1128 K (blocks
$ AGAP and AGAPT, 15 points) constrains L0 and L1 including their T
$ dependence, which is what these enthalpies would constrain.
$
$ The data, in case they are to be used some other way. Flidlider et al.
$ (1966), digitized from Figure 3 of Risold et al. (1997):
$
$    x(CaO)   X(SR)    dHmix measured   computed with the current
$                        (J/mol)          parameters (J/mol)
$    0.208    0.3960       3110              3670
$    0.496    0.2520       6570              5750
$    0.643    0.1785       5220              5360
$    0.687    0.1565       4680              5040
$
$ The scatter of the data is +-0.8 kJ/mol and the curve published by
$ Risold passes through 5750 J/mol at x(CaO)=0.5, exactly what this
$ database gives. So they are already reproduced within their own
$ scatter.
$ ==========================================================================

$ ==========================================================================
$ AGAPT - miscibility gap between 880 and 1128 K
$
$ Digitized from Figure 1 of Risold et al. (1997). The axis of that
$ figure is x(CaO), with SrO at 0 and CaO at 1.0; here it is converted
$ with
$     X(SR) = (1 - x(CaO)) / 2
$ because the Thermo-Calc X(SR) counts all atoms and half of them are
$ oxygen.
$
$ HALITE#1 is the Ca-rich branch and HALITE#2 the Sr-rich one, because
$ the macro creates the second set with SR as major constituent. Each
$ row gives the composition of ONE of the two branches; the overall
$ composition is fixed at X(SR) = 0.25, which falls inside the gap
$ throughout this temperature range.
$
$ Prior check: these 13 points fall on the binodal computed with the
$ current parameters with a mean deviation of 0.009 in mole fraction.
$ That is, the database already reproduces them.
$
$ SUM OF SQUARES TO EXPECT IF EVERYTHING CONVERGES
$   AGAPT  1.35   (13 points, the worst is that at 1113 K, residual -0.84)
$   AGAP  12.72   (2 points, with the narrow uncertainty DXG=0.0025)
$   total 14.07
$ If OPT 0 returns thousands, it means the equilibria did not converge or
$ the two composition sets collapsed; it does not mean the parameters are
$ wrong. The 12.72 of AGAP is confirmed: it is exactly what Thermo-Calc
$ reported in the run where only that equilibrium managed to compile.
$
$ The critical temperature of the gap with these parameters is 1179 K,
$ and the highest experimental point is at 1128 K. So the last equilibria
$ work at 96% of Tc, where the two branches are already close and the
$ energy surface is very flat. They are the most delicate ones.
$ ==========================================================================

$ TABLE_HEAD is NOT used here, see R8: each equilibrium needs ITS OWN
$ start values, and in a table they all share the same ones. With the
$ generic values used before (0.85 / 0.80), five of the thirteen
$ equilibria aborted with ERROR 1611 IN QTHISS: TOO MANY ITERATIONS and
$ the rest collapsed to a single phase.

$ 880 K, Sr-rich branch, x(CaO)=0.114
CREATE_NEW_EQUILIBRIUM 201,1
CHANGE_STATUS PHASE HALITE#1,HALITE#2=ENTERED 1
SET-CONDITION P=P0,T=880,N=1,X(SR)=0.25,X(O)=0.5
EXPERIMENT X(HALITE#2,SR)=0.4430:DXT
LABEL AGAPT
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#1,CA)=0.9218
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#1,SR)=0.0782
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#2,SR)=0.8872
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#2,CA)=0.1128

$ 918 K, Ca-rich branch, x(CaO)=0.906
CREATE_NEW_EQUILIBRIUM 202,1
CHANGE_STATUS PHASE HALITE#1,HALITE#2=ENTERED 1
SET-CONDITION P=P0,T=918,N=1,X(SR)=0.25,X(O)=0.5
EXPERIMENT X(HALITE#1,SR)=0.0470:DXT
LABEL AGAPT
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#1,CA)=0.9052
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#1,SR)=0.0948
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#2,SR)=0.8653
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#2,CA)=0.1347

