SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#1,CA)=1

$ ==========================================================================
$ AMLT - congruent melting points   (VERIFICATION, set weight 0)
$
$ Same construction: HALITE#1 fixed at zero, LIQUID free, and the
$ temperature comes out as the unknown because it is what is measured.
$ Conditions: P, N, X(SR), X(O) = 4, plus 1 fixed phase = 5.
$ ==========================================================================

CREATE_NEW_EQUILIBRIUM 100,1
CHANGE_STATUS PHASE LIQUID=ENTERED 1
CHANGE_STATUS PHASE HALITE#1=FIX 0
SET-CONDITION P=P0,N=1,X(SR)=0,X(O)=0.5
EXPERIMENT T=3222:25
LABEL AMLT
SET_START_VALUE Y(LIQUID,CAO)=1
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#1,CA)=1

CREATE_NEW_EQUILIBRIUM 101,1
CHANGE_STATUS PHASE LIQUID=ENTERED 1
CHANGE_STATUS PHASE HALITE#1=FIX 0
SET-CONDITION P=P0,N=1,X(SR)=0.5,X(O)=0.5
EXPERIMENT T=2870:DT
LABEL AMLT
SET_START_VALUE Y(LIQUID,SRO)=1
SET_START_VALUE Y(HALITE#1,SR)=1

$ ==========================================================================
$ DATA THAT ARE NOT INCLUDED, AND WHY
$
$ - Solubility reported by Zaitsev (1998,1999): Deffrennes concludes that
$   either they are not real measurements or they overestimate through
$   carry-over of undissolved CaO. They deviate more and more as the
$   temperature rises.
$ - Visual estimate of Bevan (1956), 14.2 at.%O at 1523 K: three times
$   any direct measurement. Discarded by every assessor.
$ - Ca activity of Zaitsev: incompatible with the Fischbach
$   solubilities. Deffrennes chooses Fischbach; using both would give a
$   fit that satisfies neither. If the activity is preferred, that work
$   gives the alternative set L(LIQUID,CA,CAO;0) = +9284.
$ - dfG(SrO) of Ono et al. (1993): the database already reproduces it,
$   -452.6 kJ/mol at 1373 K against the Risold curve at ~-453. The values
$   circulating in datos_experimentales_parrot.md were some 26 kJ/mol too
$   negative.
$ - Variation of the gap with temperature (873-1223 K): digitized from a
$   figure by a third party, unverified. Adding it would help pin down
$   the T part of L0, but it has to be checked first.
$ ==========================================================================

SAVE_WORKSPACES
